WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.768
Picture an office.

2
00:00:03.768 --> 00:00:08.454
When it's time to go home, the manager writes it on the whiteboard by the door.

3
00:00:08.454 --> 00:00:12.725
Everyone glances at the board now and then, and leaves when it says yes.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:14.705
A program does the same.

5
00:00:14.705 --> 00:00:21.252
A worker thread, one path of work beside others, checks a shared field, stop, before each small job.

6
00:00:21.252 --> 00:00:24.460
One second in, the main thread sets stop to true.

7
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:29.186
On JDK 25, three seconds later, the worker is still running.

8
00:00:29.186 --> 00:00:30.341
It never stops.

9
00:00:30.341 --> 00:00:34.421
No crash, no error, just a busy processor doing laps.

10
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.733
So here's a question that feels too simple to ask.

11
00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:38.756
Main wrote true.

12
00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:41.252
Where is the worker reading false from?

13
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.659
Most people say: the cache.

14
00:00:43.659 --> 00:00:52.420
Beside each core, the part of a processor that runs one thread, sits a cache: a small, fast memory that keeps copies of main memory.

15
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.735
The story: the worker's cache holds an old copy of stop, forever.

16
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:57.906
Let's test it.

17
00:00:57.906 --> 00:01:05.107
A switch, -Xint, turns off the JIT, the part of Java that turns busy code into fast machine code.

18
00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:06.963
The caches stay the same.

19
00:01:06.963 --> 00:01:08.968
Will the worker still hang?

20
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:15.168
It stopped at once, after about 113 million laps.

21
00:01:15.168 --> 00:01:16.711
Only the JIT changed.

22
00:01:16.711 --> 00:01:22.296
So the cache has an alibi: it was there in both runs, and only one run hung.

23
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.627
Back to the office, to find the real place.

24
00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:29.415
The whiteboard holds the numbers everyone shares: main memory.

25
00:01:29.415 --> 00:01:32.897
Each desk is a core, and whoever works there is a thread.

26
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.713
Walking to the wall is slow, so each desk keeps a photocopy of the board.

27
00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:39.456
The photocopy is the cache.

28
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:40.821
And one strict rule.

29
00:01:40.821 --> 00:01:48.971
When anyone changes a number on the board, every photocopy of it is crossed out at once, and the next look goes back to the board.

30
00:01:48.971 --> 00:01:51.918
Processors do this in hardware, in nanoseconds.

31
00:01:51.918 --> 00:01:53.673
It's called cache coherence.

32
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.737
But one place lets a write wait.

33
00:01:55.737 --> 00:01:57.493
Each desk has an outbox tray.

34
00:01:57.493 --> 00:02:03.911
A clerk drops her new number in it and goes on working, and a runner takes it to the board a moment later.

35
00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:09.004
That tray is a store buffer: a small queue where writes wait on their way to the cache.

36
00:02:09.004 --> 00:02:13.689
The catch: her next read can finish first, so her write and her read look swapped.

37
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.804
Let's catch it in the act.

38
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:18.269
Two fields, x and y, start at 0.

39
00:02:18.269 --> 00:02:21.275
Thread A writes 1 into x, then reads y.

40
00:02:21.275 --> 00:02:24.280
Thread B writes 1 into y, then reads x.

41
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.698
Shuffle the four steps like two decks of cards, keeping each thread's order.

42
00:02:29.698 --> 00:02:33.653
Whoever reads last reads after both writes, so someone sees a 1.

43
00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:35.879
Both reading 0 should be impossible.

44
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.558
The demo runs this test 524,288 times.

45
00:02:40.558 --> 00:02:44.933
In how many trials do both threads read 0?

46
00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:50.043
Run one: 240,989.

47
00:02:50.043 --> 00:02:52.552
Run two: 225,261.

48
00:02:52.552 --> 00:02:55.357
Run three: 171,281.

49
00:02:55.357 --> 00:03:01.704
For something impossible, it happens a lot.

50
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:08.890
Each thread drops its note in its own tray, then reads the other field from its photocopy.

51
00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:09.883
Zero, and zero.

52
00:03:09.883 --> 00:03:12.596
Only then do the runners reach the board.

53
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.267
With the JIT off, it still happens: 44,000 to 110,000 times.

54
00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:21.164
So this one really is the hardware.

55
00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:28.653
Make both fields volatile, a keyword for fields shared between threads, and the count is 0, every time.

56
00:03:28.653 --> 00:03:33.099
On x86 chips, Java empties the tray after each volatile write.

57
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.255
And the tray always empties within nanoseconds.

58
00:03:36.255 --> 00:03:38.270
It can't hold stop for an hour.

59
00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.089
The outbox has an alibi too.

60
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.645
One more thing sits on each desk: a notepad.

61
00:03:43.645 --> 00:03:48.559
Glancing down beats walking to the wall, so a clerk copies a number onto it.

62
00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:51.081
And no rule ever crosses a notepad out.

63
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.288
Here's what the JIT did.

64
00:03:53.288 --> 00:03:56.451
After many thousands of laps, it compiled the loop.

65
00:03:56.451 --> 00:04:02.465
Nothing in it writes stop, so it copied stop onto the notepad once, and made the rest while true.

66
00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.338
Main writes true.

67
00:04:04.338 --> 00:04:06.060
The note leaves the tray.

68
00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.438
The board says true.

69
00:04:07.438 --> 00:04:09.229
Both photocopies say true.

70
00:04:09.229 --> 00:04:12.053
And the worker keeps reading its notepad.

71
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:16.259
The notepad is a register, a tiny storage slot inside a core.

72
00:04:16.259 --> 00:04:18.473
That's where the old value lives.

73
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.597
The most focused worker in the building: it never looks up, so it never goes home.

74
00:04:23.597 --> 00:04:26.400
No rule in the hardware can reach its notepad.

75
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.375
So, the office as it stands.

76
00:04:28.375 --> 00:04:31.589
The photocopy, the cache, agrees in nanoseconds.

77
00:04:31.589 --> 00:04:35.204
The outbox, the store buffer, delivers in nanoseconds.

78
00:04:35.204 --> 00:04:38.217
The notepad, the register, stays old forever.

79
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:40.254
The picture breaks in one place.

80
00:04:40.254 --> 00:04:42.655
A clerk chooses where to look each time.

81
00:04:42.655 --> 00:04:47.036
The JIT chooses once, and the core repeats that choice billions of times.

82
00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:48.633
One warning.

83
00:04:48.633 --> 00:04:53.832
x86 chips, in most laptops, only let a later read pass an earlier write.

84
00:04:53.832 --> 00:05:00.331
ARM chips, in phones and many servers, also swap reads with reads, and writes with writes.

85
00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:04.434
A clean run on your laptop only proves your laptop is polite.

86
00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:12.436
So write to Java's rules, the Java Memory Model: another thread's write is promised only when something links the threads.

87
00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:16.006
For our flag, the link is one word: volatile.

88
00:05:16.006 --> 00:05:22.759
The JIT can't keep a volatile field on the notepad, so every pass reads it again, and the worker goes home.

89
00:05:23.467 --> 00:05:29.148
Java on the left, Kotlin on the right, where the annotation @Volatile does the same job.

90
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:33.448
So the old value never lived in the cache, or the outbox.

91
00:05:33.448 --> 00:05:37.217
It lived on the notepad, because nothing linked the threads.

92
00:05:37.217 --> 00:05:39.919
A shared plain field comes with no promise.

93
00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:40.987
Make it volatile.

94
00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:41.927
Quick check.

95
00:05:41.927 --> 00:05:45.857
A loop hangs with the JIT on, and stops with the JIT off.

96
00:05:45.857 --> 00:05:47.925
Photocopy, outbox, or notepad?
